==Description==

'''Macro MeshResetOrigin''' est une macro qui consolide les transformations des maillages en réinitialisant l'origine de chaque maillage sélectionné à l'origine du document, '''sans déplacer la géométrie'''. Elle est destinée à être utilisée après une opération d’alignement (par exemple, ''Édition → Aligner sur…'' sur des objets de Mesh), afin d’intégrer l’alignement obtenu dans les coordonnées du maillage plutôt que de le conserver sous forme de décalage du [[Placement.fr|placement]] ou de s’appuyer sur un conteneur parent.

Chaque élément {{Incode|Mesh::Feature}} sélectionné est traité comme suit :

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
# Read its current Placement (the transform that positions it in the world).
# Apply that placement matrix to a copy of the mesh, so every vertex moves into the position it currently occupies in world space.
# Reassign the transformed mesh and reset the Placement property to identity (0,0,0 / 0°).
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
The mesh does not move on screen; only its origin now coincides with the world origin, since the transform lives in the mesh geometry instead of in the Placement property. The whole operation is wrapped in a single undo transaction, so one '''Undo''' reverts everything.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Objects that are not meshes, and meshes that live inside a parent container (e.g. [[Std_Part|App::Part]]), are skipped with a warning printed to the [[Report_View|Report View]]. Meshes already at the origin are left untouched.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
==Location==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Mesh workbench (external/optional macro).
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
==Usage==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
# Select one or more Mesh objects in the tree view or the 3D view.
# Run the macro.
# Each mesh's origin is reset to the world origin; the geometry does not move.
# Check the Report view for a per-mesh summary (reset / already at origin / skipped).
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
{{Emphasis|If a mesh is inside an App::Part, drag it out of the container first, then run the macro.}}
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
==Example==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
A typical workflow is aligning a 3D scan mesh to the world axes to make it easier to work with:
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
# Import a 3D scan mesh.
# Create (or reuse) a reference geometry to align the mesh on, for example a plane.
# Select both the reference object and the mesh, then go to ''Edit → Align To...''.
# Pick at least 3 points on the face of the mesh to flatten, then at least 3 points on the reference face to align with, then click ''Align''.
# The mesh is now aligned, but the Placement/transform is no longer trivial to work with.
# Run '''MeshResetOrigin''' on the mesh: it does not move, but its origin is now aligned with the world.
# Iterate as needed to refine the alignment — particularly useful when working with 3D-scan meshes or 3D-printer STL files.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
A step-by-step illustrated walkthrough is available on the [https://github.com/de-seingalt/MeshResetOrigin GitHub repository].
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
==Notes==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
* Requires FreeCAD 1.1.1 or newer (not tested on earlier versions).
* Meshes with a parent [[Std_Part|App::Part]] container are skipped; remove them from the container first.
</div>

